WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.313
Every business turns inputs into output. Workers give their time, which is called labour, and the business makes goods or services from it.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:24.247
Imagine two students washing cars at a school fair. Ali washes twelve cars in three hours. Sara washes twelve cars in four hours. They wash the same number, but who works faster?

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:38.158
Divide the cars by the hours. Ali washes four cars per hour. Sara washes three cars per hour. Ali is more productive: he gets more output from each hour of work.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:48.519
Labour productivity equals total output divided by the labour input. The labour input is either the hours worked or the number of workers.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.698
So the method is: find the total output, find the labour input, then divide and state the unit.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:08.128
The unit matters. Per hour means divide by hours. Per worker means divide by workers. For example, five hundred chairs made by twenty-five workers is twenty chairs per worker.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:16.832
Why does this matter? Higher productivity means more output from the same labour, so the wage cost of each unit falls.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:29.639
Here is a real question from Paper two in November twenty twenty-five. Calculate the factory's productivity of smartphones per hour. It is worth two marks.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:39.733
The factory makes one thousand smartphones in twenty hours. One thousand divided by twenty is fifty. So productivity is fifty smartphones per hour.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:51.270
A common mistake is dividing upside down: hours divided by output. That gives a tiny number, which tells you something has gone wrong.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:02:00.058
Other students multiply instead of dividing, or give the number with no unit. Divide, then write the full unit.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:14.936
Now you try. Six bakers make seven hundred and twenty loaves in an eight-hour shift. Calculate the labour productivity in loaves per worker. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:31.980
The question says per worker, so divide by the six bakers. Seven hundred and twenty divided by six is one hundred and twenty loaves per worker. Check: one hundred and twenty times six is seven hundred and twenty. The eight-hour shift is not needed.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:41.676
Remember the method: find the output, find the labour input, then divide and state the unit. Now try the practice questions for this type.
